## Why This Is Asked
Understanding vector clocks and the happens-before relation for debugging causality in distributed systems.

## Key Concepts
- Vector clocks
- Happens-before
- Concurrency detection in logs

## Code Example
```javascript
function compareVC(a, b) {
  let lessEq = true, greaterEq = true;
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (a[i] > b[i]) lessEq = false;
    if (a[i] < b[i]) greaterEq = false;
  }
  if (lessEq && greaterEq) return 'equal';
  if (lessEq) return 'a_before_b';
  if (greaterEq) return 'b_before_a';
  return 'concurrent';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you integrate vector clocks with a log-aggregation system to detect causality violations?
- What are the storage and performance implications for large-scale deployments?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about combined implications and negation, and to design a small exhaustive search with pruning.

## Key Concepts

- Implication and negation in boolean constraints
- Exhaustive search with pruning
- Small-scope backtracking for correctness checks

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve() {
  for (let A = 0; A <= 1; A++) {
    for (let B = 0; B <= 1; B++) {
      for (let C = 0; C <= 1; C++) {
        const ok1 = (!A) || B;     // A -> B
        const ok2 = (!B) || (!C);  // B -> ¬C
        const ok3 = (A || B || C); // at least one true
        if (ok1 && ok2 && ok3) return {A,B,C};
      }
    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of least fixed-point computation in monotone boolean networks with thresholds and how to implement an efficient propagation algorithm that converges even with cycles.

## Key Concepts
- Monotone operators and least fixed points
- Threshold-based activation with in-neighbor counting
- Queue-driven BFS-like propagation
- Handling cycles via convergence to a fixpoint

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation of the propagation
function leastFixedPoint(T, edges, thresh) {
  const n = thresh.length;
  const succ = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u,v] of edges) succ[u].push(v);
  const count = Array(n).fill(0);
  const inSet = Array(n).fill(false);
  const q = [];
  for (const i of T) { inSet[i] = true; q.push(i); }
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const v of succ[u]) {
      count[v]++;
      if (!inSet[v] && count[v] >= thresh[v]) {
        inSet[v] = true;
        q.push(v);
      }
    }
  }
  return Array.from({length: n}, (_,i) => i).filter(i => inSet[i]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for weighted thresholds or non-monotone edges?
- How would you prove the result is the least fixed point containing T?

